{"context":"Anvil is a reusable platform designed to serve multiple consumers across different frameworks, environments, and use cases. This introduces a fundamental architectural challenge: how to prevent responsibilities from leaking across layers.\n\nWhen responsibility boundaries are not explicitly defined, the following problems emerge:\n\n**Core becomes coupled to consumers.** Features shaped by one project's needs become part of the core, making it harder for other projects to adopt the platform. Over time, the core reflects the priorities of its first consumer rather than the requirements of a general-purpose toolkit.\n\n**Framework assumptions become implicit.** Convenience functions for a specific framework — database migrations, configuration caching, asset compilation — migrate into the core bec Source: ADR-002-core-architecture-and-domain-boundaries.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil adopts a layered architecture with four explicit architectural domains:\n\n1. **Core** — the reusable platform\n2. **Framework Adapters** — framework-specific behavior\n3. **Applications** — consumer projects that use Anvil\n4. **External Platforms** — systems that Anvil interacts with\n\nEvery capability MUST belong to exactly one domain.\n\nCapabilities MAY NOT span multiple domains.\n\nCross-domain leakage — behavior that belongs in one domain but is implemented in another — is prohibited unless explicitly documented and accepted by a future ADR.\n\nThe boundaries between domains are established by this ADR and MUST NOT be weakened by future decisions.","consequences":"### 10.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Framework independence.** The Core can be adopted by projects using any framework. Framework-specific needs are handled by adapters without modifying the Core.\n\n**Stable architecture.** Domain boundaries provide a stable foundation. The Core evolves through deliberate additions, not accidental accretions.\n\n**Simpler maintenance.** Each domain has 